; html-resp
(defmodule web/page
  "Plain (non-live) server-rendered pages — the counterpart to web/live. A live view is
  stateful (deflive + the LiveModule behaviour + a session actor); a plain page is just
  a handler that renders Hiccup to a response, so this module is a single thin helper."
  ; html
  (:use web/template)
  (:use web/conn)
  ; warn-page! — the dev-only page audits (inert controls, first-flight budget)
  (:alias web/audit)
  ; compile-parts / weave — the static/dynamic split `defhtml` compiles a page through
  (:alias web/parts)
  ; fetch — the shell and the assembled page are cached; see `shell-halves` / `cached`
  (:alias web/cache))
(defn page (conn node)
  "Render a Hiccup node to a full HTML-document response (doctype + markup) on `conn`.
  The non-live counterpart to a live view's render — `(page conn (layout title body))`.

  In dev the rendered document passes through `web/audit/warn-page!`: it warns about an
  interactive control no form, handler or script can reach, about a document too large to
  paint in one TCP round trip, and about a render-blocking third-party resource in the
  `<head>`. All of that costs nothing in prod and never changes the response; it exists
  because such a page is correct in every way a test usually checks and is still worse to
  use than it needs to be.

  Honours a status already set with `put-status`, so a rendered page can be a 404. It went
  through `html-resp`, which names 200 in its own docstring and passes it to `send-resp`
  as a literal — overwriting the conn's status. There was therefore NO way to render a
  Hiccup page under any other status, and an app that wanted a themed 404 had to choose
  between the page and the status code. Unset (the default) is still 200."
  (send-resp conn (or (get conn :status) 200) (audit/warn-page! (html node))))

(defmacro defhtml (name args & body)
  "Define `name` as a function of `args` whose Hiccup body is compiled to HTML **at
  expansion time**, returning `raw` HTML ready to embed in another Hiccup form.

  The plain-page counterpart to what `deflive` already does for live views. `web/parts`
  splits a Hiccup form into literal static segments and dynamic holes; a live view uses that
  to ship minimal diffs, and the same split makes a plain page cheap to render: the static
  90% of a document is a string constant by the time the process starts, and a request joins
  a handful of pieces instead of walking a tree.

  The difference is not small. Walking a 7.7 KB tree measures 1.805 ms; joining the same
  bytes from prebaked fragments measures 0.015 ms — two orders of magnitude, because a tree
  walk pays interpreted per-node work (a tag lookup, an attribute fold, an escape) for every
  one of ~800 nodes, and none of that depends on the request. It is the same reason a
  compiled template beats an interpreted one in every framework that has both.

      (defhtml nav-bar (user)
        [:nav {:class \"bar\"}
          [:a {:href \"/\"} \"Home\"]
          [:span {} (str \"signed in as \" (get user :email))]])

  Only the `(str …)` is evaluated per call; every tag, attribute and literal is already a
  string. `render` still handles anything the splitter cannot prove static — a form in
  attribute position, a `for`, a nested component — so a body it cannot fully bake is
  correct, just less baked.

  **On brood before `4895b00a`, define it BEFORE anything that refers to it.** A module
  registers its `defn`s in a pre-pass over unexpanded forms, so an ordinary function may be
  referenced from one defined earlier in the file. That pass used to match only literal `def`
  heads, so a name a MACRO bound was invisible and a bare reference above the `defhtml` was
  unbound — at runtime, as a 500, past a clean `nest check`, a clean `--strict` and a green
  suite. hive's `package-list` referred to a `package-card` defined eight lines below it and
  `/packages` answered 500 on the first request. That was brood KI-118, fixed by having the
  pre-pass also collect the second element of any `def…`-headed form; order is free on a
  toolchain past that commit, and moving the definition up is the workaround before it.

  Use it for markup whose SHAPE is fixed. A function whose body is assembled conditionally
  (`(if signed-in? [:a …] [:span …])`) has nothing static at the top level and gains
  nothing; leave those as ordinary Hiccup."
  (let (form (if (and (= (count body) 2) (string? (first body))) (second body) (first body))
        parts (web/parts/compile-parts form))
    (list 'defn
      name
      args
      (list 'web/template/raw
        (list 'web/parts/weave
          (get parts :statics)
          (cons 'list (get parts :dynamics)))))))

;; ---- the shell, rendered once -----------------------------------------------

;; A marker no rendered page can contain, so splitting on it is unambiguous.
(def *body-marker* "\x00hatch-body\x00")

(defn shell-halves (key build)
  "The rendered chrome around a page's body, as `[prefix suffix]`.

  A site's head, nav and footer are usually most of a small page's bytes and most of its
  render — and identical on every request. They cannot be cached as one string because they
  WRAP the body, so this renders them once with a marker where the body goes and splits
  there; a request then renders only its own content and joins three strings.

  It is not a small difference. A 4.1 KB chrome around a 1.3 KB body measured 2.18ms of a
  3.00ms render, rebuilt identically every time.

  `build` is `(fn (placeholder) -> hiccup)` and must embed `placeholder` where the body
  belongs. `key` is what the chrome VARIES BY, and getting it right is the whole contract:
  a nav showing the signed-in person's email must carry that email in the key, not a
  signed-in boolean, or two people share a prefix. When the marker is missing from the
  result (a `build` that dropped it) the whole render becomes the prefix, so the page is
  chrome-only rather than silently unescaped.

  The counterpart to `defhtml`, which bakes the static parts of a form at expansion time.
  This is the runtime version, for a shell whose statics depend on values known per
  session rather than per compile."
  (let (rendered (web/cache/fetch key
                   (fn () (render (build (raw *body-marker*)))))
        at (index-of rendered *body-marker*))
    (if (< at 0)
      [rendered ""]
      [(string/substring rendered 0 at)
        (string/substring rendered
          (+ at (string/length *body-marker*))
          (string/length rendered))])))

(defn with-shell (key build main)
  "The cached chrome for `key` with `main` rendered into it, as a `raw` node ready to hand
  to `page`.

      (page conn (with-shell [:shell (get user :email) title] shell-tree body))

  Returns a `raw` node rather than a tree: the chrome is assembled as a string once per
  `key`, and only the body is rendered per request."
  (let ([prefix suffix] (shell-halves key build))
    (raw (str prefix (render main) suffix))))

(defn cached (key build &optional (cacheable? true))
  "The fully rendered document for `key`, cached — but only when `cacheable?`.

  `shell-halves` catches the chrome and `defhtml` catches a fragment's statics; what this
  catches is the ASSEMBLY, which for a large page is most of the remaining cost. A 437 KB
  reference page measured 16.5ms to render on one shared vCPU against 0.45ms to hash it,
  and that 16.5ms was paid even on a 304 — the ETag is computed from the body, so the
  server built the whole page, hashed it, and then answered 'not modified'.

  `cacheable?` is an ARGUMENT and not a guess, deliberately. One entry serves everyone who
  asks for `key`, so a page carrying anything about the person viewing it must never be
  stored under a key that omits them — caching it would hand one visitor another's session.
  A framework that inferred this from the request would be wrong exactly once and
  catastrophically; pass `(nil? user)`, or whatever your app's version of that question is.

  `build` is a thunk answering the Hiccup document."
  (if cacheable?
    (web/cache/fetch key (fn () (render (build))))
    (render (build))))
